{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass in the fortieth year, in the eleventh month, on the first of the month.</b> This teaches that he did not reprove them except close to death. From whom did he learn? From Jacob, who did not reprove his sons except close to death. He said: Reuben my son, I say to you — for what reason did I not reprove you all these years? So that you would not abandon me and go and attach yourself to Esau my brother. And for four reasons a person is not reprove except close to death: so that he not reprove him and again reprove him, and so that his fellow not see him and be shamed before him, and so forth, as is found in Sifrei. And so Joshua did not reprove Israel except close to death, and so Samuel, as it says (I Samuel 12), ״הִנְנִי עֲנוּ בִי״, and so David with Solomon his son (I Kings 2)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:45:20.649561253Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}